course: dictionary
lesson: 12231
title: Big, but not exact
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Considerable means large, or quite a lot. It is a formal word, so you will often meet it in reports, news and exams. When you see it, ask: is something big here? The words around it give you the clue. «The trip cost a considerable amount of money.» The amount is big, so considerable means a lot.
explain: Look at the words after it. «The bridge took considerable time to build.» Building a bridge takes a long time, so considerable time means quite a lot of time. Check your meaning by swapping in "a lot of": "a lot of time" still makes sense.
explain: In maths word problems, considerable tells you the number is large but not exact. The problem does not give you the exact count, so you must use the other numbers in the question to work out the answer.
fact: Considerable means large in size, amount or importance, or quite a lot.
fact: «She has a considerable talent for drawing» means she has a lot of talent, and it is important to her.
fact: Common phrases include considerable amount, considerable time, at considerable cost and to a considerable extent.
fact: «The house was damaged to a considerable extent» means it was damaged quite badly.
fact: Considerable is a formal word, so you will find it more often in writing than in everyday talk.
exercise: «The trip cost a considerable amount of money.» What does considerable mean here? | Tiny, hardly any | Exactly ten pounds | A borrowed amount | *A large amount, quite a lot
exercise: «Ravi spent a considerable time practising the piano.» What does considerable time suggest? | A very short time | No time at all | A time in the past | *Quite a long time
exercise: Mum says the new road will be built at considerable cost. What does considerable cost mean? | A small cost, like a penny | *A large cost, a lot of money | A cost paid by the school | Free of charge
test: «The storm caused considerable damage to the town.» What does considerable damage mean? | Damage to a single tree | A little scratch on a wall | No damage at all | *Quite a lot of damage
test: Why might a writer choose considerable instead of big in a report? | It means small, so it is good for tiny things | *It is a formal word that suits writing about important things | It means the same as tiny | It is a word only young children use
exam: A maths question says, «A considerable number of children joined the club.» What does this tell you? | The number is exactly 12 | *The number is large, but not exact | The number is small and exact | There were no children
